## 6. Scenario C — Reduced CPU contention / memory-pressure experiment

`loadtest.py` now supports explicit per-category worker counts
(`--cpu-workers`, `--memory-workers`, `--io-workers`, `--mixed-workers`,
`--idle-workers`, each accepting `0` to disable that category). Use these to
build a genuinely memory-focused scenario instead of just turning CPU workers
down — a run with `--oversub 0.3` alone still spawns the default I/O and
mixed workers too (2 disk workers, 2 mixed workers), so it isn't isolating
memory on its own. To actually isolate memory pressure:

```bash
# Terminal 1 — memory workers only, everything else disabled
python3 loadtest.py --duration 120 --cpu-workers 0 --io-workers 0 --mixed-workers 0 --idle-workers 0 --memory-workers 6

# Terminal 3
watch -n1 'vmstat 1 2 | tail -1'   # watch si/so columns specifically

# Terminal 2 (start ~5s later)
pgbench -c 10 -j 2 -T 90 -P 5 bankforge
```

`--memory-workers 6` cycles through the 100/200/300MB sizes twice (six
workers, ~1.2GB total) — adjust upward if your lab VM has enough RAM that a
smaller allocation doesn't create visible pressure; the goal is to get
`free -m`'s `available` column noticeably lower than idle, not necessarily
to force swapping.

If you instead want a **reduced-CPU-contention** variant (some CPU noise,
but less than the default 2.5x oversubscription, alongside the normal
disk/mixed/idle workers) — which is what the earlier `--oversub 0.3` command
actually produces — that's a legitimate scenario too, just a different one:

```bash
python3 loadtest.py --duration 120 --oversub 0.3
```

Don't call this "memory pressure only" in your notes — it's reduced CPU
contention with the normal memory/disk/mixed workers still present. Use the
explicit `--*-workers 0` flags above when the experiment specifically needs
to isolate one resource category.

Check `si`/`so` (swap in/out) in `vmstat` — with default `vm.swappiness=60`
under real pressure you may see these turn non-zero; with `vm.swappiness=1`
they should stay near zero. For the THP portion of this scenario, check
`cat /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled` before/after disabling it
and look for `pgbench` interval latency spikes that don't correlate with any
CPU or I/O signal — that disconnect is the THP signature.

