## 9. Quick reference — all experiments

| Scenario | Command | What it isolates |
|---|---|---|
| Baseline | `pgbench` alone, no `loadtest.py` | Control measurement |
| A — general contention | `loadtest.py --duration 120` | Combined CPU/memory/disk noise |
| B — dirty page buildup | `loadtest.py --duration 120 --no-fsync` | `vm.dirty_ratio` / checkpoint stalls |
| C1 — memory pressure only | `loadtest.py --duration 120 --cpu-workers 0 --io-workers 0 --mixed-workers 0 --idle-workers 0 --memory-workers 6` | `vm.swappiness` / THP, isolated |
| C2 — reduced CPU contention | `loadtest.py --duration 120 --oversub 0.3` | Lighter CPU noise alongside normal disk/mixed/idle workers |

Run each scenario twice — once against default `sysctl` values, once after
applying the relevant fix from M-OS-02 — and you have a complete, data-backed
before/after story for the module.

---

## 10. Recording results — five numbers per experiment, not one

The classic mistake in this kind of lab is: "I changed the parameter and TPS
went up, therefore the parameter was the fix." That's a correlation, not a
proven cause. Have students capture **five numbers**, not just `tps`, for
every experiment — the OS-level metric has to move in the direction that
explains the PostgreSQL-level result before you credit the change:

```
                    BASELINE       TUNED
------------------------------------------------
pgbench TPS         ________       ________
Latency avg          ________       ________
CPU utilization      ________       ________
I/O w_await           ________       ________
Dirty: (peak)        ________       ________
```

Then apply this check before accepting a tuning change as the real fix:

```
        Did pgbench TPS/latency improve?
                     |
                    YES
                     |
     Did the matching OS metric also improve?
   (Dirty: down for Step 2, si/so down for Step 3, etc.)
                     |
                    YES
                     |
              Keep the change — you've
           established causality, not just
                   correlation
```

If TPS improved but the OS metric didn't move in the expected direction,
something else changed between runs (background process, cache warm-up,
system noise) — rerun the experiment before crediting the sysctl change.
This is the difference between "I tuned Postgres" and "I tuned Postgres and
can prove why it worked."
